<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaiser</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaiser is the German word for emperor, derived from the Roman name Caesar. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> it was the title of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, first held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> from 1871. The Kaiser controlled foreign policy and the army and appointed the chancellor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaiser.jpg" alt="Kaiser" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrayal_of_Franz_Joseph_and_his_wife_Elisabeth_on_a_millennium_memorial_leaf_with_the_crown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Kaiser is derived directly from the Roman emperors' title of Caesar, which came from the personal name of the Julii Caesares, a branch of the gens Julia to which Gaius Julius Caesar belonged. It has been suggested, on the strength of an anecdote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, that Caesar himself once used his cognomen as a title, but this is thought unlikely. The Hindi and Urdu Kaisar-i-Hind, used of the British Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, shares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin but comes through Persian Kaysar, not from the German word.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a> called themselves Kaiser, combining the imperial title with that of King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, which the designated heir took before the imperial coronation. They saw their rule as a continuation of that of the Roman emperors. From 1452 to 1806, except in 1742 to 1745, only members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> family held the title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> was dissolved in 1806, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> kept the title, and from 1804 their head was Kaiser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1867 the empire became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, with the emperor also King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Four Habsburg kaisers ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>: Franz I (1804 to 1835), Ferdinand I (1835 to 1848), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> (1848 to 1916) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i">Karl I</a> (1916 to 1918). In central Europe Franz Joseph is still remembered with high respect as Der Kaiser, because of his long reign before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was unified without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1871 there was debate over the monarch's title. The first kaiser preferred Kaiser von Deutschland, Emperor of Germany, but Chancellor Bismarck's choice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">Deutscher Kaiser</a>, German Emperor, was adopted, since it did not claim rule over all German lands and so would not offend Austria. Three Hohenzollern kaisers held it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> (1871 to 1888), Friedrich III, who ruled for 99 days in 1888, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> (1888 to 1918), in whose reign the monarchy ended near the close of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> anti-German feeling gave the word, especially as applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, strongly negative associations in English-speaking countries. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> it entered everyday speech through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>: Kaiserwetter is sunny weather, linked to the sunshine on his birthday on 18 August, and Kaiserschmarrn is a sweetened pancake named for him. Kaiserjäger and Kaiserschützen were elite units of the Austro-Hungarian army. Der Kaiser is also the nickname of the footballer Franz Beckenbauer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaiser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peters, named Reichskommissar for the Kilimanjaro Region in 1891 under Soden, was recalled in 1892 and in 1897, after three investigations, deprived of his commission, his title and his pension; <b>Kaiser</b> Wilhelm II nevertheless gave him the title of 'retired Reichskommissar' in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reichstag was elected by universal male suffrage and voted the budget, but it could not appoint or remove the chancellor, who remained responsible to the <b>Kaiser</b> alone.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November 1918 Philipp Scheidemann of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the German Republic at the Reichstag building, the abdication of <b>Kaiser</b> Wilhelm II was announced, and Prince Max of Baden handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the German Empire of 1871, however, Otto von Bismarck rejected the British model: the chancellor answered to the <b>Kaiser</b>, not to the Reichstag.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…sang it entering Worms for the Diet in April 1521 (Heinrich Heine); that it honoured Luther's friend Leonhard <b>Kaiser</b>, executed on 16 August 1527 (K. F. T. Schneider); that the Lutheran princes sang it entering Augsburg for the Diet of 1530 (Jean-Henri Merle d'Aubigné); or that it belongs…</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>With revolution spreading, Chancellor Max von Baden announced the <b>Kaiser</b>'s abdication and Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic in Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
